 "edits": [
  {
   "op": "replace_brief",
   "brief": "cholestasis",
   "html": "<div class=\"brief sp\" id=\"cholestasis\" data-entry=\"hub\" data-lens=\"fm\" data-spine=\"2\" data-shelf=\"fm\" data-bp=\"gi\">\n<h4>Abnormal Liver Tests</h4>\n<p class=\"sub\">hepatocellular vs cholestatic &middot; liver or bone source &middot; ducts on ultrasound &middot; biliary vs sclerosing cholangitis<span class=\"subq\">Which pattern is it, and are the ducts dilated on ultrasound?</span></p>\n<nav class=\"sp-step\" aria-label=\"Decision spine\"><a href=\"#chol-s1\"><b>1</b><span>Script</span></a><a href=\"#chol-s2\"><b>2</b><span>Urgent</span></a><a href=\"#chol-s3\"><b>3</b><span>Differential</span></a><a href=\"#chol-s4\"><b>4</b><span>Workup</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>5</b><span>Criteria</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>6</b><span>Severity</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>7</b><span>Treatment</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>8</b><span>Follow-up</span></a><a href=\"#chol-bank\" class=\"sp-prac\"><span>Practice</span></a></nav>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"chol-s1\"><b class=\"sp-n\">1</b>Presentation</h5>\n<div class=\"dp\"><span class=\"lbl\">Bottom line</span><ul class=\"bl\"><li>Sort the pattern before chasing causes: <b>AST and ALT high</b> is hepatocellular; <b>alkaline phosphatase (ALP) high with direct bilirubin</b> is cholestatic; <b>bilirubin alone</b> is an isolated hyperbilirubinemia.</li><li>An ALP rise with normal aminotransferases needs a <b>gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT)</b>: a high GGT puts the source in the liver; a <b>normal GGT</b> means bone, growth or placenta.</li><li>A cholestatic pattern goes to <b>right upper quadrant ultrasound first</b>: <b>dilated ducts</b> mean extrahepatic obstruction, imaged next by magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography (MRCP) or CT; <b>nondilated ducts</b> mean intrahepatic disease, tested next with the <b>antimitochondrial antibody</b>.</li></ul></div>\n<p>Injured hepatocytes leak aminotransferases; blocked bile flow raises ALP and backs conjugated bilirubin into the blood. Excess unconjugated bilirubin comes from overproduction (hemolysis) or weak conjugation (Gilbert syndrome).</p>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"chol-s2\"><b class=\"sp-n\">2</b>Urgent risks</h5>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>Act now</caption><thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fever, right upper quadrant pain and jaundice (Charcot triad); hypotension and confusion may add the Reynolds pentad</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Acute (ascending) cholangitis: blood cultures and antibiotics, then endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) to remove the stone as soon as possible.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Painless jaundice with weight loss and a palpable nontender gallbladder (Courvoisier sign)</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Pancreatic head cancer until excluded: abdominal CT.</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"chol-s3\"><b class=\"sp-n\">3</b>Differential</h5>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>Cholestatic causes</caption><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Decides it</th><th>Confirm with</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Choledocholithiasis</td><td><span>Biliary pain with a <b>dilated common bile duct</b>; ALP, GGT and direct bilirubin often up.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Ultrasound; MRCP if unclear.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Acute cholangitis (ascending cholangitis)</td><td><span>Charcot triad on top of a duct stone.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Act now (step 2) owns it.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Pancreatic head cancer</td><td><span><b>Painless</b> jaundice, weight loss, <b>Courvoisier sign</b>; a dilated duct ending at a head mass.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Abdominal CT.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Drug-induced cholestasis</td><td><span>ALP and bilirubin up after <b>amoxicillin-clavulanate, anabolic steroids, chlorpromazine or oral contraceptives</b>.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Timing after the drug; tests usually improve once it is withdrawn.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Sarcoidosis (infiltrative liver disease)</td><td><span>Cholestasis with <b>bilateral hilar lymph nodes and hypercalcemia</b>; portal granulomas.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Liver biopsy shows the granulomas.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"liver-preg\">Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy</button></td><td><span>Pregnancy with intense itching, worst at night, and raised aminotransferases.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Liver Disease in Pregnancy owns the workup and delivery timing.</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two chronic bile duct diseases to tell apart: primary biliary cholangitis (PBC), formerly primary biliary cirrhosis, and primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC).</p>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>PBC or PSC</caption><thead><tr><th>Axis</th><th>PBC</th><th>PSC</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Patient</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Middle-aged women with itching and fatigue</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Men, young to middle-aged, with inflammatory bowel disease <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-pop\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"chol-pop-1\">In practice</button><span class=\"sp-pop-b\" id=\"chol-pop-1\" hidden>The Merck Sharp and Dohme (MSD) Manual gives a peak age at diagnosis of 25 to 45 years, about two-thirds men, and inflammatory bowel disease in 60 to 90%.</span></span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Antibody</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Antimitochondrial antibody (AMA), positive in 90 to 95%</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">p-ANCA (perinuclear antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody); AMA negative</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Association</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Sjögren syndrome, Hashimoto thyroiditis, rheumatoid arthritis, celiac disease</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Ulcerative colitis in about 90%; screening colonoscopy at diagnosis</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Ducts and histology</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Small intrahepatic ducts, so MRCP looks normal; destructive cholangitis, with granulomas in some</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>Beading</b> of intra- and extrahepatic ducts on MRCP; onion-skin periductal fibrosis</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Course</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Jaundice and xanthelasma often come late</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Raised risk of cholangiocarcinoma, gallbladder cancer and, with colitis, colon cancer</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>Hepatocellular pattern: AST and ALT lead</caption><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Clue or first test</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Viral hepatitis</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Serologies first</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Alcohol-related liver disease</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">AST:ALT above 2</span></span></td></tr><tr><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"masld\">Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD), formerly nonalcoholic fatty liver disease</button></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">ALT above AST</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Autoimmune hepatitis</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Anti-smooth muscle antibody (type 1) or anti-liver-kidney microsomal type 1 antibody (type 2)</span></span></td></tr><tr><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-wilson\">Wilson disease</button></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Low ceruloplasmin, high urinary copper</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Hereditary hemochromatosis</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Iron studies first</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Serum alpha-1 antitrypsin level, with genotype or phenotype testing</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Drug-induced liver injury</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Timing after the drug; stop it</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Cause still unclear</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Liver biopsy</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>Isolated bilirubin or isolated ALP</caption><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Decides it</th><th>Next best step</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gilbert syndrome</td><td><span><b>Indirect</b> bilirubin up alone, rising with fasting, stress or illness; mildly low conjugating enzyme.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Fractionate; normal CBC, smear and reticulocytes settle it.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Hemolysis</td><td><span>Indirect bilirubin up with a high reticulocyte count.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">CBC, smear, reticulocyte count.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Crigler-Najjar syndrome</td><td><span>Indirect bilirubin from absent (type I) or reduced (type II) conjugating enzyme.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Give a phenobarbital trial: type II, the less severe form, responds.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Dubin-Johnson or Rotor syndrome</td><td><span><b>Direct</b> bilirubin up with normal liver enzymes and CBC; Dubin-Johnson has a black liver.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">No further testing: the pattern is benign.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Bone or placental ALP</td><td><span>ALP up with a <b>normal GGT</b>: Paget disease (the commonest isolated rise after 40), a growing child, pregnancy, bone metastases, vitamin D deficiency, a healing fracture.</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Order calcium, phosphate, vitamin D and bone imaging.</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"chol-s4\"><b class=\"sp-n\">4</b>Workup</h5>\n<figure class=\"lcp w5a\"><p class=\"kick\">Diagnostic workup</p><p class=\"w5z\">First step</p><div class=\"w5t first\"><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Any abnormal liver test</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Hepatic panel with fractionated bilirubin</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">AST and ALT lead</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">Hepatocellular: serologies and the first tests in the step 3 hepatocellular table</span></div><div class=\"w5fd\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">ALP and direct bilirubin lead</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m\"><span class=\"mask\">Cholestatic: ultrasound next</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Bilirubin alone</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Indirect: CBC, smear, reticulocytes; direct with normal enzymes: Dubin-Johnson or Rotor</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">ALP up, aminotransferases normal</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">GGT</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">GGT high</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">Hepatic: work up as cholestatic</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">GGT normal</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Bone or placenta: calcium, phosphate, vitamin D and bone imaging</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Cholestatic pattern</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Right upper quadrant ultrasound</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Ducts dilated</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">Extrahepatic: MRCP or CT</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Ducts not dilated</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Intrahepatic: antimitochondrial antibody</span></div></div></div><p class=\"w5z\">Targeted tests</p><div class=\"w5t\"><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Nondilated ducts in a middle-aged woman with itching</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Antimitochondrial antibody</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Positive</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">PBC: biopsy usually not needed</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Negative</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">MRCP, then biopsy if still unexplained</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Dilated duct with biliary pain</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">MRCP if ultrasound is unclear</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Filling defect in the common bile duct</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Choledocholithiasis</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Dilated duct, painless jaundice, weight loss</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Abdominal CT</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Mass where the dilated duct ends</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Pancreatic cancer</span></div></div></div><p class=\"w5z\">Diagnostic test</p><div class=\"w5t\"><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Inflammatory bowel disease with a cholestatic pattern</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">MRCP</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Beading of intra- and extrahepatic ducts</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">PSC; colonoscopy if colitis is not yet known</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Cause still unclear after antibodies and imaging</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Liver biopsy</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Granulomas, duct destruction or drug injury</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Sarcoidosis, AMA-negative PBC or drug-induced injury</span></div></div></div><p class=\"w5skip\"><span class=\"w5z\">Skip</span><span class=\"mask\">ERCP to diagnose: MRCP shows the ducts without procedural risk; ERCP is kept for removing a stone, opening a stricture or taking tissue.</span></p></figure>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>Treatment by cause</caption><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Treat</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Choledocholithiasis</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">ERCP with sphincterotomy to remove the stone; cholecystectomy afterward.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Acute cholangitis</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Act now (step 2) owns it.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>PBC</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Ursodeoxycholic acid (ursodiol) first line; it slows damage and delays transplant. A second agent if the response is poor. <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-pop\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"chol-pop-2\">In practice</button><span class=\"sp-pop-b\" id=\"chol-pop-2\" hidden>The MSD Manual says a peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor (PPAR) agonist, including some fibrates, may be used second line; the board cards name obeticholic acid.</span></span></span></td></tr><tr><td>PSC</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">No drug clearly improves survival (ursodiol evidence is mixed); ERCP dilation and stenting for a dominant stricture; liver transplant for end-stage disease.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Drug-induced cholestasis</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Withdraw the drug early; most recover.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"liver-preg\">Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy</button></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Ursodiol first line; delivery timing in Liver Disease in Pregnancy.</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Gilbert, Dubin-Johnson or Rotor syndrome</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Reassure; no treatment.</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"sp-pause\"><b class=\"pp-t\">Pause point</b>\n<div class=\"pp-b\">\n<span class=\"sl\">Liver tests: pattern first</span>\n<span class=\"pp-a\">&rarr; <span class=\"mask\">Any cholestatic pattern gets an ultrasound</span></span>\n</div>\n<div class=\"pp-b\">\n<span class=\"sl\">Duct size on the first image</span>\n<span class=\"pp-a\">&rarr; <span class=\"mask\">Dilated: MRCP or CT; nondilated: the antimitochondrial antibody</span></span>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n<h5 class=\"authored-hdr\" id=\"chol-bank\">Practice</h5>\n<ol class=\"bank\">\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-d1=\"ERCP first\" data-d2=\"Repeat ultrasound in 3 months\" data-item-id=\"q_0950bfff255e50f9aa43\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_5c65913635d853e89742\" data-d1-id=\"o_31629d185aeb5840b3e5\" data-d2-id=\"o_aba5e26374ef5c04b18f\" data-key-why=\"A head mass behind a dilated duct is imaged and staged with abdominal CT first.\" data-d1-why=\"ERCP is kept for removing a stone, opening a stricture or taking tissue, not the first look at a mass.\" data-d2-why=\"Repeating the ultrasound later delays the diagnosis of a likely cancer.\">Ultrasound shows a <b>dilated common bile duct and a pancreatic head mass</b> → <b>CT abdomen</b> → pancreatic adenocarcinoma</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Primary sclerosing cholangitis\" data-d2=\"Autoimmune hepatitis\" data-item-id=\"q_2def7a94c1c8561f86e1\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_a94414694b7658eeb579\" data-d1-id=\"o_2375542f4dc6501e8304\" data-d2-id=\"o_3d5999a345195005ad9c\" data-key-why=\"Nondilated ducts with a positive antimitochondrial antibody in a woman with itching is primary biliary cholangitis.\" data-d1-why=\"Primary sclerosing cholangitis is a man with inflammatory bowel disease and beading on MRCP; AMA is negative.\" data-d2-why=\"Autoimmune hepatitis is hepatocellular, with anti-smooth muscle antibody.\">Normal ducts + <b>AMA positive</b> in a woman with pruritus → <b>Primary biliary cholangitis</b> → ursodiol</li>\n<li data-type=\"test\" data-d1=\"ERCP\" data-d2=\"Liver biopsy\" data-item-id=\"q_10e9b3319d655586bdce\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_ff03d09eb70555388840\" data-d1-id=\"o_15d5c549f8fe51f69525\" data-d2-id=\"o_fb0104bc26f65ce2983f\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnostic study to obtain at this time?\" data-key-why=\"MRCP shows the beading of primary sclerosing cholangitis without procedural risk.\" data-d1-why=\"ERCP is kept for a stricture that needs dilation, a stent or tissue.\" data-d2-why=\"Biopsy is for disease that MRCP cannot show.\">Young man with <b>ulcerative colitis</b> and a cholestatic pattern → <b>MRCP</b> &rarr; beads on a string → PSC</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Cholestatic liver injury\" data-d2=\"Bile duct obstruction\" data-item-id=\"q_08b946e4e4205f6cb045\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_8c6a2d4b35965ea18d24\" data-d1-id=\"o_ee24ae5505055bb5a637\" data-d2-id=\"o_ad1b9e19c0ca53418dc5\" data-key-why=\"A normal GGT places the ALP outside the liver: bone, growth or placenta.\" data-d1-why=\"Cholestatic liver injury raises GGT along with ALP.\" data-d2-why=\"Duct obstruction raises GGT and direct bilirubin.\">Alk phos elevated with a <b>normal GGT</b> → <b>Bone source or pregnancy</b> &rarr;  not liver disease at all</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Acute cholecystitis\" data-d2=\"Choledocholithiasis without cholangitis\" data-item-id=\"q_9f5d29acb26253f79858\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_487ef968bd0b5ecfbc40\" data-d1-id=\"o_75a763fa7d5e5326a874\" data-d2-id=\"o_68dbc483e9fb581e8b92\" data-key-why=\"Fever, right upper quadrant pain and jaundice together are the Charcot triad of acute cholangitis.\" data-d1-why=\"Acute cholecystitis is a blocked cystic duct; the common duct stays open, so jaundice is not part of it.\" data-d2-why=\"A duct stone without fever is choledocholithiasis; fever on top makes it cholangitis.\"><b>Charcot triad</b> (fever + right upper quadrant pain + jaundice) → Ascending cholangitis → <b>urgent ERCP</b></li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Choledocholithiasis\" data-d2=\"Primary biliary cholangitis\" data-item-id=\"q_08de329f5e5a59dd851e\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_7ac9bfc44688528c8834\" data-d1-id=\"o_8a2af2f4a33b554eb239\" data-d2-id=\"o_e5f4e737ef6b5a4fa3ad\" data-key-why=\"Painless jaundice, weight loss and a palpable nontender gallbladder (Courvoisier sign) point to a pancreatic head tumor.\" data-d1-why=\"A duct stone causes biliary pain.\" data-d2-why=\"Primary biliary cholangitis leaves the ducts nondilated.\"><b>Painless jaundice + weight loss + palpable nontender gallbladder</b> (<b>Courvoisier sign</b>) → Pancreatic head malignancy &rarr;  not stones</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Crigler-Najjar type II\" data-d2=\"Hemolysis\" data-item-id=\"q_0be8ac71bebf5e328ace\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_1318925ba5f55bd9bfb3\" data-d1-id=\"o_74a68e3208b859b58e18\" data-d2-id=\"o_f8359025be9e5e8aa474\" data-key-why=\"Mild indirect bilirubin that rises with fasting or illness, with everything else normal, is Gilbert syndrome.\" data-d1-why=\"Crigler-Najjar type II is a reduced conjugating enzyme, not a mild fasting rise.\" data-d2-why=\"Hemolysis brings a high reticulocyte count.\">Isolated <b>indirect</b> hyperbilirubinemia appearing with fasting or illness, all else normal → <b>Gilbert syndrome</b> → reassurance</li>\n<li data-type=\"next\" data-d1=\"Start ursodiol\" data-d2=\"Liver biopsy\" data-item-id=\"q_698a6d92f12459929956\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_32840acb1d615ca78387\" data-d1-id=\"o_7bef7476cfbd59a2ab79\" data-d2-id=\"o_bc0cb3e469e95ecd95ae\" data-key-why=\"Drug-induced cholestasis usually recovers when the drug is withdrawn early.\" data-d1-why=\"Ursodiol treats primary biliary cholangitis, not a reversible drug reaction.\" data-d2-why=\"Biopsy is for a cause still unclear after antibodies and imaging.\">Cholestatic pattern after <b>amoxicillin-clavulanate</b>, anabolic steroids, oral contraceptives, or chlorpromazine → Stop the drug, then monitor</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Primary biliary cholangitis\" data-d2=\"Lymphoma\" data-item-id=\"q_888d45f50c345f178c35\" data-item-version=\"1\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_835582bec7aa580ca59f\" data-d1-id=\"o_c4864611c36b56cd9d3d\" data-d2-id=\"o_e918807e1bbf5718aec4\" data-key-why=\"Cholestasis with bilateral hilar lymph nodes and hypercalcemia is sarcoidosis with portal granulomas.\" data-d1-why=\"Primary biliary cholangitis brings no hilar nodes or hypercalcemia.\" data-d2-why=\"Liver granulomas with bilateral hilar nodes fit sarcoidosis, not lymphoma.\">Cholestasis + hilar lymphadenopathy + hypercalcemia → <b>Sarcoidosis</b></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"danger\"><span class=\"lbl\">Distractors</span>\n<b>Anti-smooth muscle antibody and iron studies</b> look for hepatocellular disease, the <b>wrong pattern</b> for a cholestatic picture. <b>ERCP</b> waits until imaging shows a lesion to treat (the step 4 Skip line). Repeating the <b>liver tests</b> later fits only once a reversible cause has been found and removed.</div>\n<div class=\"rule\"><span class=\"lbl\">Transferable rule</span> Use the <b>liver test pattern</b> as a <b>triage instrument</b> to choose tests. Tests are pattern-specific: an autoimmune hepatitis antibody looks for hepatocellular disease and does not belong in the workup of a cholestatic pattern.</div>\n<div class=\"traps\"><span class=\"pill p-seq\">Sequencing trap</span></div>\n</div>",
